"subs": [
{
"t": 2.05,
"dur": 2.101,
"text": "…four hundred twelve. Four hundred thirteen."
},
{
"t": 4.451,
"dur": 2.099,
"text": "Wh… who's there? What are you counting?"
},
{
"t": 6.8,
"dur": 2.07,
"text": "Counting what's ours, sir. Almost done."
},
{
"t": 9.87,
"dur": 6.853,
"text": "Back again, huh? This week, the old quarter. A family home three generations old, and a jar nobody's opened."
},
{
"t": 16.974,
"dur": 3.626,
"text": "And one kid guarding it. So well-behaved. Creepy well-behaved."
},
{
"t": 20.85,
"dur": 1.852,
"text": "One month earlier. Let it tell you."
},
{
"t": 23.201,
"dur": 5.755,
"text": "I'm this home's ma xo — a kitchen-corner ghost. I've lived by the stove since great-great-grandpa's time."
},
{
"t": 29.206,
"dur": 5.973,
"text": "Great-great-grandpa gave me one job: our whole fortune's in the jar under the kitchen. Guard it. So I did."
},
{
"t": 35.429,
"dur": 7.09,
"text": "Welcome! A three-generation home, keeping the old quarter's soul alive. Two million dong a night. Altar photos, fifty thousand dong extra."
},
{
"t": 42.769,
"dur": 7.17,
"text": "Keeping the old quarter's soul alive by charging admission to see an altar. Great-great-grandpa would charge you too."
},
{
"t": 50.34,
"dur": 6.643,
"text": "Sixty billion dong. Gut the whole inside. Keep the mossy gate for Western tourists to photograph as heritage."
},
{
"t": 57.233,
"dur": 2.124,
"text": "Sign! Gut the kitchen too, for all I care."
},
{
"t": 59.608,
"dur": 3.322,
"text": "Sell the house, and the house's things leave. I have to keep them here."
},
{
"t": 63.43,
"dur": 5.146,
"text": "First inspection: the door locked itself. The surveyor spent two hours in the bathroom."
},
{
"t": 68.825,
"dur": 7.514,
"text": "Second inspection: the house was thirty feet deep, but the surveyor couldn't reach the back wall. He checked the time. Two a.m."
},
{
"t": 76.589,
"dur": 4.44,
"text": "A Western guest left a one-star review: “A kid counted money all night.”"
},
{
"t": 81.279,
"dur": 2.207,
"text": "I'm not scaring anyone. I'm just counting."
},
{
"t": 83.985,
"dur": 3.183,
"text": "Hey, ma xo. Sign and I'll cut you in. Here. Banana rice cake offering."
},
{
"t": 87.419,
"dur": 3.073,
"text": "I don't take bribes, sir. I only eat rice cake offerings."
},
{
"t": 92.391,
"dur": 3.46,
"text": "Takes the offering, skips the job. Now that's a gig I want."
},
{
"t": 96.352,
"dur": 4.625,
"text": "We're selling the house, okay? Great-great-grandpa's jar under the kitchen isn't part of the deal!"
},
{
"t": 101.227,
"dur": 1.925,
"text": "It's just an earthenware jar. Keep it."
},
{
"t": 103.403,
"dur": 9.774,
"text": "Nobody opened it for three generations. The family was the richest on the block for three generations. To Mr. Khai, the jar could only hold gold."
},
{
"t": 113.426,
"dur": 4.452,
"text": "Add a line. The jar under the floor belongs to the seller. All of it, including what's inside."
},
{
"t": 118.128,
"dur": 6.904,
"text": "We open the jar Saturday morning, take inventory, then see the notary. You put down six billion dong. I'll hold it."
},
{
"t": 125.282,
"dur": 2.027,
"text": "Remember that line. It'll come back."
},
{
"t": 127.909,
"dur": 8.226,
"text": "Friday night, Mr. Khai sneaked into the kitchen with a shovel. He'd take the gold first, then leave the buyers an empty jar in the morning."
},
{
"t": 136.386,
"dur": 3.261,
"text": "He went into the storage room for a pickaxe. The door shut."
},
{
"t": 139.896,
"dur": 0.962,
"text": "…one. Two. Three."
},
{
"t": 141.359,
"dur": 1.563,
"text": "Open up! I own this house!"
},
{
"t": 143.172,
"dur": 5.746,
"text": "I know, sir. Selling a house means taking inventory. Great-great-grandpa said so. I'll count it for you."
},
{
"t": 149.168,
"dur": 2.769,
"text": "…four hundred twelve. Four hundred thirteen. That's all."
},
{
"t": 153.937,
"dur": 8.344,
"text": "Saturday morning. The storage room door opened by itself. They dug up the jar in front of the buyers, their lawyer, and half the neighborhood."
},
{
"t": 162.531,
"dur": 2.613,
"text": "Four hundred thirteen papers. I.O.U.s, sir."
},
{
"t": 165.394,
"dur": 6.444,
"text": "Great-great-grandpa borrowed to buy land. Grandpa borrowed to cover the interest. Your dad borrowed to refinance."
},
{
"t": 172.088,
"dur": 4.358,
"text": "Three generations. Not one payment. Every time, they put up… this very house."
},
{
"t": 177.445,
"dur": 5.06,
"text": "The lenders live right here. It's the old quarter. Every house still has the paperwork."
},
{
"t": 182.756,
"dur": 4.607,
"text": "A notary can't sign off on a disputed house. We're out. You forfeit the deposit."
},
{
"t": 187.613,
"dur": 5.987,
"text": "Oh, and that addendum you wrote yourself? The jar and everything inside belong to you. Congratulations."
},
{
"t": 193.85,
"dur": 1.356,
"text": "So… what do I have left?"
},
{
"t": 195.607,
"dur": 0.832,
"text": "The jar, sir."
},
{
"t": 197.238,
"dur": 5.709,
"text": "Mr. Khai stopped selling the house. Now he sells tickets to see the jar. Twice the crowd."
},
{
"t": 203.198,
"dur": 5.311,
"text": "I've guarded this family's things for three generations. They're the richest family on the block… in debt."
},
{
"t": 209.009,
"dur": 6.553,
"text": "Three hundred years on top of a cabinet taught me one thing: if nobody dares open a jar, don't open it."
},
{
"t": 216.063,
"dur": 7.422,
"text": "Next time: an apartment family doesn't notice their kid's been missing for three days… because everyone's glued to their phone."
},
{
"t": 223.735,
"dur": 6.254,
"text": "Hit subscribe. Or tonight, something will sit behind your cabinet, counting the videos you haven't watched."
}
],
